import OddPerfect.ExcessFourReduction
import OddPerfect.TwoExcEngineTail

/-!
# Total excess at least 4: exclusion S2b

Let `N` be an odd perfect number with special prime `q` and exponent 2 at 3, at most three
primes `r₁`, `r₂`, `r₃` of exponent 4, every other prime factor except `q` of exponent 2. This
module proves that no such `N` exists (`excessS2b : ExcessS2b`, the statement of
`ExcessFourReduction`). This concerns only this restricted family, not odd perfect numbers in
general. Every index in this family is 3 or 5 and 3 divides N, so this exclusion also follows
from the published theorem of Fletcher, Nielsen and Ochem (Math. Comp. 81, 2012); it is proved
here independently by a kernel checked certificate.

## The proof

The proof is a kernel checked certificate for the checker `checkV` of `OddPerfect.TwoExcEngine`,
a tool whose soundness theorem `checkV_sound` is proved in `TwoExcEngineCheck` and
`TwoExcEngineTail` (`defSound`). The certificate `cert_ex_S2b_100000_nolanded_ruleL.jsonl`
(sha256 `12fd8d079b8bbfe3bc42ac877bbfe33bb911d6016333ec22cf6fe0257802b4c2`, 235 lines) is the
output of an exact search. The checker parameters allow 3 exceptional primes (the prime 3 is a
square key), every exception other than 3 of exponent exactly 4, and do not use the landed
facts. As a checker term it has 234 records, 49 contradiction leaves, 64 abundant leaves, and
121 forced nodes. The term is checked in one kernel evaluation; primality enters only through
the verified oracle `prS2b`, which the checker asks about 30 primes (25 below 4096, covered by
the verified table `primeB`; the others by one flat Pratt certificate of 10 entries and a search
tree over its primes). The bridge `famV_S2b` turns the hypotheses of `ExcessS2b` into the family
of the parameters.

Generated by `gen_excess.py` from `cert_ex_S2b_100000_nolanded_ruleL.jsonl`.

Landed theorems used: the tail bound of the deficient leaves, through `defSound` and
`RankinTailGeneral`; nothing else beyond the engine.
-/

